{"qid":"test_Marketing_161","dataset":"mmmupro","question":"A psychologist is interested in testing whether there is a difference in the distribution of personality types for business majors and social science majors. The results of the study are shown in Table 11.49. Conduct a test of homogeneity. Test at a 5% level of significance. At the 5% significance level, what can you conclude?\nOptions:\nA: The distribution of personality types for social science majors is significantly more diverse than for business majors.\nB: There is insufficient evidence to conclude that the distribution of personality types is different for business and social science majors.\nC: There is a significant difference in the personality types of social science majors, but not business majors.\nD: The distribution of personality types for business majors is significantly more diverse than for social science majors.\nE: The differences in personality types between business and social science majors are not statistically significant.\nF: There is a significant difference in the personality types of business majors, but not social science majors.\nG: Not certain.\nH: The distribution of personality types is identical for both business and social science majors.\nI: There is sufficient evidence to conclude that the distribution of personality types is different for business and social science majors.\nAnswer with the option's letter.","gold":"B","category":"Marketing","generated_text":"To conduct a test of homogeneity, we are testing whether the distribution of personality types (Open, Conscientious, Extrovert, Agreeable, Neurotic) is the same across the two groups: Business majors and Social Science majors.\n\nThe null hypothesis (H\u2080) is that the distributions are the same across the two groups.\nThe alternative hypothesis (H\u2081) is that the distributions are different.\n\nWe will use the chi-square test for homogeneity.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the expected frequencies.\n\nTotal number of observations = 41 + 52 + 46 + 61 + 58 + 72 + 75 + 63 + 80 + 65 = 583\n\nRow totals:\n- Business: 41 + 52 + 46 + 61 + 58 = 258\n- Social Science: 72 + 75 + 63 + 80 + 65 = 355\n\nColumn totals:\n- Open: 41 + 72 = 113\n- Conscientious: 52 + 75 = 127\n- Extrovert: 46 + 63 = 109\n- Agreeable: 61 + 80 = 141\n- Neurotic: 58 + 65 = 123\n\nExpected frequency for each cell = (Row total * Column total) / Grand total\n\nExpected for Business, Open: (258 * 113) / 583 = 29,054 / 583 \u2248 50.17\nExpected for Business, Conscientious: (258 * 127) / 583 = 32,766 / 583 \u2248 56.20\nExpected for Business, Extrovert: (258 * 109) / 583 = 28,122 / 583 \u2248 48.22\nExpected for Business, Agreeable: (258 * 141) / 583 = 36,438 / 583 \u2248 62.50\nExpected for Business, Neurotic: (258 * 123) / 583 = 31,734 / 583 \u2248 54.43\n\nExpected for Social Science, Open: (355 * 113) / 583 = 40,315 / 583 \u2248 69.10\nExpected for Social Science, Conscientious: (355 * 127) / 583 = 45,235 / 583 \u2248 77.90\nExpected for Social Science, Extrovert: (355 * 109) / 583 = 38,655 / 583 \u2248 66.30\nExpected for Social Science, Agreeable: (355 * 141) / 583 = 50,055 / 583 \u2248 86.10\nExpected for Social Science, Neurotic: (355 * 123) / 583 = 43,765 / 583 \u2248 75.40\n\nStep 2: Calculate the chi-square statistic.\n\n\u03c7\u00b2 = \u03a3 [(Observed - Expected)\u00b2 / Expected]\n\nFor Business, Open: (41 - 50.17)\u00b2 / 50.17 = (-9.17)\u00b2 / 50.17 = 84.0889 / 50.17 \u2248 1.676\nFor Business, Conscientious: (52 - 56.20)\u00b2 / 56.20 = (-4.20)\u00b2 / 56.20 = 17.64 / 56.20 \u2248 0.314\nFor Business, Extrovert: (46 - 48.22)\u00b2 / 48.22 = (-2.22)\u00b2 / 48.22 = 4.9284 / 48.22 \u2248 0.102\nFor Business, Agreeable: (61 - 62.50)\u00b2 / 62.50 = (-1.50)\u00b2 / 62.50 = 2.25 / 62.50 \u2248 0.036\nFor Business, Neurotic: (58 - 54.43)\u00b2 / 54.43 = (3.57)\u00b2 / 54.43 = 12.7449 / 54.43 \u2248 0.234\n\nFor Social Science, Open: (72 - 69.10)\u00b2 / 69.10 = (2.90)\u00b2 / 69.10 = 8.41 / 69.10 \u2248 0.121\nFor Social Science, Conscientious: (75 - 77.90)\u00b2 / 77.90 = (-2.90)\u00b2 / 77.90 = 8.41 / 77.90 \u2248 0.108\nFor Social Science, Extrovert: (63 - 66.30)\u00b2 / 66.30 = (-3.30)\u00b2 / 66.30 = 10.89 / 66.30 \u2248 0.164\nFor Social Science, Agreeable: (80 - 86.10)\u00b2 / 86.10 = (-6.10)\u00b2 / 86.10 = 37.21 / 86.10 \u2248 0.432\nFor Social Science, Neurotic: (65 - 75.40)\u00b2 / 75.40 = (-10.40)\u00b2 / 75.40 = 108.16 / 75.40 \u2248 1.434\n\nSumming these up:\n\u03c7\u00b2 \u2248 1.676 + 0.314 + 0.102 + 0.036 + 0.234 + 0.121 + 0.108 + 0.164 + 0.432 + 1.434 = 4.611\n\nStep 3: Determine degrees of freedom.\n\ndf = (number of rows - 1) * (number of columns - 1) = (2 - 1) * (5 - 1) = 1 * 4 = 4\n\nStep 4: Find the critical value.\n\nAt \u03b1 = 0.05 and df = 4, the critical value from the chi-square table is 9.488.\n\nStep 5: Compare and make a decision.\n\nSince the calculated \u03c7\u00b2 (4.611) is less than the critical value (9.488), we fail to reject the null hypothesis.\n\nConclusion: There is insufficient evidence to conclude that the distribution of personality types is different for business and social science majors.\n\nThis matches option B.\n\nFinal answer: B","n_input":585,"n_generated":1685,"seq_len":2270,"grid":[8,38],"img_span":[4,308],"user_text_span":[309,580],"answer_seg":[1683,1684],"ans_tp":[2267],"texts":["<|im_start|>","user","\n","<|vision_start|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|im